                       <#review-5> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Selection. 40 regions of the human brain that introductory psychology and neuroscience courses name, from the four lobes of the cerebral cortex and its best-known areas (prefrontal, orbitofrontal and anterior cingulate cortex, the primary motor, somatosensory, auditory and visual cortex, Broca's and Wernicke's areas, the angular gyrus, the fusiform face area, the insula) through the limbic and memory structures (hippocampus, entorhinal cortex, amygdala, mammillary bodies), the diencephalon (thalamus, hypothalamus, suprachiasmatic nucleus, pineal gland), the olfactory bulb, the basal ganglia and reward system (striatum, nucleus accumbens, substantia nigra, ventral tegmental area), to the brainstem (midbrain, superior colliculus, periaqueductal gray, pons, medulla oblongata, reticular formation, locus coeruleus, raphe nuclei), the cerebellum and the corpus callosum. The compiler wrote the list down without taking it from any source."@en ,
                 "Items and names (front). Each card names the Wikidata item it is about (dcterms:subject), checked by its class (P31/P279), description and MeSH ID (P486). Each front is the usual name of the region in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French: a Wikidata label or alias of the item, a Svensk MeSH preferred term or synonym for Swedish, or the title of the Wikipedia article in that language linked from the item; the evidence for each name was recorded. Where sources disagreed, the term used in that language's textbooks and encyclopedias was preferred. Every front begins with a capital letter."@en ,
                 "Functions and locations (back and note). The back names the region's main functions in a few words, the note where it is and, for some, one more fact or a caveat. Both were written for the deck from facts in Wikidata, NLM's MeSH scope notes and trees, Svensk MeSH and NINDS, each claim backed by a quote from a fetched source, with Wikipedia used to verify only. Functions are simplified for learners: most functions involve networks of regions, and the notes avoid popular myths (a lone \"fear center\" or \"pleasure center\", left-brain and right-brain types). The Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French backs and notes were translated from the English and checked by translating them back."@en ,
                 "Fetching. Every request used the project's own User-Agent (\"SolidMemoDeckResearch/1.0 (https://github.com/solid-memo/website)\"), only on the hosts listed as sources, and only where their robots.txt allowed."@en ,
                 "Uniqueness. Every front is unique in each of the six languages and every card is about its own Wikidata item; the deck is studied front to back."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0-1.0. Its content comes from sources that allow it: Wikidata and Svensk MeSH (CC0), and NLM's MeSH and NINDS's fact sheet (works of the U.S. Government); NLM is credited as the source of the MeSH data. Names of brain regions are terms, not wording. The backs and notes were written for the deck, and a review compared each with the English Wikipedia article on its region and reworded every text that came close to it; Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0) was used to verify facts only."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Source research and fact check per card group"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 40 cards in eight groups: each Wikidata item, its class, labels, aliases, sitelinks and MeSH ID, each front name in six languages, and every claim of the backs and notes, checked against freshly fetched sources by a checker trying to refute them."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 24 findings: wrong or overstated claims (such as damage that \"causes\" an aphasia where the sources say \"is associated with\"), imprecise locations and translations that drifted from the English; all were checked against the sources and fixed or declined with a reason."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language: one review per language"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every card's front, back and note in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French, against the English and the established terms of each language, doubtful names checked against Wikidata and that language's Wikipedia."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 91 findings (English 15, Swedish 20, Korean 12, German 8, Spanish 5, French 31): mainly terms written two ways across cards, French spacing before semicolons and colons, two Swedish notes that said diencephalon for midbrain, and grammar; each was checked and applied or declined with a reason."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing and attribution"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The English backs and notes of all 40 cards, compared with their evidence and with the full English Wikipedia article on each region."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 14 findings: two texts nearly copied Wikipedia (the periaqueductal gray note, the thalamus back) and nine were close paraphrases or rested on facts found only in Wikipedia; all were reworded from the allowed sources."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Consistency across the deck"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 40 cards: style, length, capitalization and separators, duplicate fronts in any language, backs too alike to tell apart, notes that contradict each other, duplicate Wikidata items."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 30 findings, mostly terms written two ways and notes without a subject; no duplicate fronts or items. Across rounds 0–3, 101 findings were applied and 37 declined with a reason, often because another finding's wording was better supported."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review, round 1 (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 40 cards in six languages; 25 Wikidata items fetched again."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 12 errors and warnings, all fixed: an unattested Swedish name for the primary auditory cortex, French spacing in four backs, three orbitofrontal backs out of style, and a German term used two ways."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-25T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review, round 2 (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 40 cards in six languages; all 40 Wikidata items fetched again, with their classes and labels."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every front has evidence, every item is the right region and the facts hold. Three warnings, all fixed: German used two names for the medulla and two for the lateral sulcus; the German temporal-lobe and insula notes then got the attested term \"Sulcus lateralis\" too."@en .

